WEBVTT

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.650
Transport Layer Security is one of the basic trust mechanisms behind modern web

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:05.280
communication.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.530
When a browser connects to a site, an application calls an API,

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:13.045
a mobile app talks to a backend, or one service calls another inside a cloud environment,

00:00:13.045 --> 00:00:17.680
TLS is often the protocol that provides confidentiality, integrity, and authentication.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.125
It also appears at load balancers, API gateways, reverse proxies, SaaS platforms,

00:00:22.125 --> 00:00:24.100
ingress controllers, service meshes,

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.950
and internal applications that never face the public internet.

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:31.155
Post-quantum cryptography matters here because TLS relies on public-key cryptography

00:00:31.155 --> 00:00:32.990
during important parts of the handshake.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:36.755
Public-key mechanisms help authenticate the server and establish key material.

00:00:36.755 --> 00:00:38.770
Once the handshake has done its job,

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:43.345
TLS commonly uses symmetric encryption to protect the application data moving across the

00:00:43.345 --> 00:00:43.855
connection.

00:00:43.855 --> 00:00:45.005
That split is important:

00:00:45.005 --> 00:00:48.020
the quantum concern is most urgent for some public-key algorithms,

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.925
while symmetric encryption and hashing have different planning considerations.

00:00:51.925 --> 00:00:53.864
The risk is not limited to a future outage.

00:00:53.864 --> 00:00:57.840
Harvest-now, decrypt-later scenarios matter when traffic is sensitive for years.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.230
An adversary may collect encrypted network traffic today and attempt to decrypt it later

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:05.340
if the public-key protection used during the session becomes breakable.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.755
This is especially relevant for regulated information, government data,

00:01:08.755 --> 00:01:11.185
sensitive business records, intellectual property,

00:01:11.185 --> 00:01:15.015
and enterprise API traffic where confidentiality has a long lifetime.

00:01:15.015 --> 00:01:18.720
For TLS and web services, PQC is a migration and operations problem,

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.380
not just a cryptography problem.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:25.050
The work touches endpoint inventory, certificates, libraries, clients, mobile apps,

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:28.530
proxies, monitoring, performance, change management, vendor readiness,

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:29.610
and rollback planning.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:34.780
A useful program treats PQC as practical crypto agility: understand where TLS is used,

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.760
prioritize based on risk, test standards-based mechanisms,

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.040
and make change possible without breaking critical services.
